WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.884
An ordinance is a law made

2
00:00:01.884 --> 00:00:03.770
while Parliament is in recess.

3
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.298
Can a government just keep

4
00:00:05.298 --> 00:00:05.911
renewing it?

5
00:00:06.411 --> 00:00:07.158
No.

6
00:00:07.458 --> 00:00:08.850
The Supreme Court called

7
00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.765
that a fraud on the Constitution.

8
00:00:11.265 --> 00:00:13.564
Under Article 123, an ordinance

9
00:00:13.564 --> 00:00:15.937
must be laid before both Houses.

10
00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:17.535
It ceases six weeks after

11
00:00:17.535 --> 00:00:18.894
Parliament reassembles,

12
00:00:18.994 --> 00:00:20.581
or sooner, if both Houses

13
00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:21.469
disapprove it.

14
00:00:21.919 --> 00:00:24.500
In Krishna Kumar Singh, in 2017,

15
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.490
a seven-judge bench examined

16
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.245
ordinances re-issued again

17
00:00:28.245 --> 00:00:29.528
and again in Bihar.

18
00:00:29.978 --> 00:00:32.386
The majority held that re-promulgation

19
00:00:32.386 --> 00:00:34.351
is a fraud on the Constitution,

20
00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.002
that laying an ordinance

21
00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.566
before the legislature is

22
00:00:37.566 --> 00:00:38.192
mandatory,

23
00:00:38.292 --> 00:00:39.797
and that the satisfaction

24
00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:42.025
behind it is open to judicial review.

25
00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:44.139
So an ordinance is a bridge,

26
00:00:44.289 --> 00:00:45.399
not a road.

27
00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:46.773
Your turn.

28
00:00:46.923 --> 00:00:48.563
What did Krishna Kumar Singh

29
00:00:48.563 --> 00:00:50.145
hold about re-promulgation?
